Chapter: RvArgOrder
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The smallest thing that can tell whether a two-argument call places its
 arguments in the declared order. Subtraction is not commutative, so the
 answer names the order: seven minus two is five, and two minus seven is
 negative five. No recursion, no match, no tail call, no list.

 The second probe pairs a literal with a call, because that is the shape
 the parameter-order defect was first seen in and it takes a different
 branch of the two-argument emitter than two bare literals do.

Section: Probes

  f-two : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  f-two (a) (b) = a - b

  seven : Integer
  seven = 7

 The same predicate that orders call arguments also drives `skip-save` in
 the binary-operand emitter, so a binary expression whose right operand is a
 nullary definition is the second shape it governs. Subtraction again, so
 the answer names the order rather than merely differing from it.

 `f-bin` is deliberately NOT the discriminating case and is kept as the
 control: `x` is a parameter, so it sits in a callee-saved register and a
 call cannot reach it. `f-bin2` is the real one. Its left operand is an
 expression, so it lands in a caller-saved temp, and the call to `seven` on
 the right is then free to destroy it before the subtraction reads it.

  f-bin : Integer -> Integer
  f-bin (x) = x - seven

  f-bin2 : Integer -> Integer
  f-bin2 (x) = (x * 2) - seven

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("lit-lit: " & show (f-two 7 2))
   print-line-uni ("call-lit: " & show (f-two seven 2))
   print-line-uni ("lit-call: " & show (f-two 2 seven))
   print-line-uni ("bin-call: " & show (f-bin 10))
   print-line-uni ("bin2-call: " & show (f-bin2 10))
  end
